1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,500
[Merc sings up from C and stops on ti]

2
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:10,886
TAVI: It just hangs there, waiting.
Is that because we started on C?

3
00:00:11,236 --> 00:00:14,610
MERC: Start on D, and listen.
Will it still hang?

4
00:00:14,910 --> 00:00:16,410
[pause and predict]

5
00:00:16,710 --> 00:00:20,610
[Tavi sings up from D and stops on ti]

6
00:00:21,710 --> 00:00:25,160
MERC: Still waiting. Now the last step.

7
00:00:25,260 --> 00:00:27,160
[Tavi sings the last step, up to do]

8
00:00:27,560 --> 00:00:30,004
TAVI: So home moved with the start.

9
00:00:30,087 --> 00:00:35,059
Then why does do-re-mi sound
finished when it lands on do,

10
00:00:35,142 --> 00:00:38,018
and restless when it stops on ti?

11
00:00:39,418 --> 00:00:42,618
[chime]

12
00:00:42,818 --> 00:00:46,525
MERC: By the end,
you'll build a major scale from its steps,

13
00:00:46,608 --> 00:00:52,257
start it on any note, name its notes,
and hear ti lean towards home.

14
00:00:52,857 --> 00:00:57,546
First, one from Lesson 2.
How many semitones make an octave?

15
00:00:57,896 --> 00:00:59,696
[think]

16
00:00:59,996 --> 00:01:04,780
Twelve. Seven letters,
and five notes between them.

17
00:01:05,130 --> 00:01:09,614
But a major scale climbs that
octave in just seven steps.

18
00:01:10,114 --> 00:01:13,251
TAVI: Twelve semitones in seven steps?

19
00:01:13,334 --> 00:01:16,444
Then some steps must
be bigger than others?

20
00:01:16,794 --> 00:01:18,044
MERC: Let's measure them.

21
00:01:19,012 --> 00:01:24,480
Here's that do-re-mi, from C,
on the white keys: a major scale.

22
00:01:24,830 --> 00:01:28,880
[the white keys from C up to C]

23
00:01:29,280 --> 00:01:35,667
Now measure each step, as in Lesson 2.
With a black key in between, it's a tone.

24
00:01:36,167 --> 00:01:42,539
TAVI: And with no black key in between,
like E to F, it's a semitone?

25
00:01:42,889 --> 00:01:45,780
MERC: Yes. Watch each step as you listen.

26
00:01:46,130 --> 00:01:52,930
[the same scale, slowly, step by step]

27
00:01:53,330 --> 00:02:00,965
Tone, tone, semitone.
Tone, tone, tone, semitone.

28
00:02:01,315 --> 00:02:07,404
The steps aren't equal.
The two short ones are E to F, and B to C.

29
00:02:08,004 --> 00:02:12,179
TAVI: The short steps look scattered.
Is there a pattern to them?

30
00:02:12,529 --> 00:02:16,058
MERC: Split the scale in two,
and listen to each half.

31
00:02:16,408 --> 00:02:18,658
[C, D, E, F]

32
00:02:19,158 --> 00:02:21,408
[G, A, B, C]

33
00:02:21,808 --> 00:02:29,795
Both halves climb the same way: tone,
tone, semitone. One more tone joins them.

34
00:02:30,145 --> 00:02:37,846
Now count it in semitones.
Two, two, one. Two. Two, two, one.

35
00:02:38,346 --> 00:02:39,528
TAVI: That's twelve.

36
00:02:39,611 --> 00:02:43,360
Is that why it ends on C again,
an octave up?

37
00:02:43,710 --> 00:02:44,647
MERC: Exactly.

38
00:02:44,730 --> 00:02:49,090
Seven steps that add up to twelve
semitones: one octave.

39
00:02:51,990 --> 00:02:57,600
TAVI: Same white keys, just starting on D.
Surely that's the same scale?

40
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:05,325
MERC: Predict: the white keys from D,
up to the next D. Is it still do-re-mi?

41
00:03:05,725 --> 00:03:08,725
[pause and predict]

42
00:03:09,075 --> 00:03:11,698
First, the white keys from D.

43
00:03:12,048 --> 00:03:16,098
[the white keys from D up to D]

44
00:03:16,598 --> 00:03:19,328
Now the major pattern, from D.

45
00:03:19,678 --> 00:03:23,728
[the major pattern from D]

46
00:03:24,228 --> 00:03:27,355
It changes. The white keys climb tone,

47
00:03:27,438 --> 00:03:32,070
semitone, tone, tone,
tone, semitone, tone.

48
00:03:32,420 --> 00:03:35,297
The short steps fall in different places,

49
00:03:35,380 --> 00:03:39,225
and the third and seventh
notes come out a semitone low.

50
00:03:39,625 --> 00:03:45,062
Keeping the pattern takes two black keys:
F sharp and C sharp.

51
00:03:45,145 --> 00:03:48,908
That's D major,
named after its first note.

52
00:03:49,508 --> 00:03:53,355
TAVI: So a major scale
isn't a set of keys at all.

53
00:03:53,438 --> 00:03:54,718
It's the pattern?

54
00:03:55,068 --> 00:04:01,742
MERC: Yes. Start the pattern on any of the
twelve notes, and you get a major scale.

55
00:04:02,142 --> 00:04:05,382
[a major scale from G]

56
00:04:05,882 --> 00:04:09,122
[a major scale from E]

57
00:04:09,522 --> 00:04:13,707
The shape never changes.
Only the keys under it change.

58
00:04:14,057 --> 00:04:18,484
Every start except C needs
at least one black key.

59
00:04:18,567 --> 00:04:22,120
C is just the one that
fits the white keys alone.

60
00:04:25,020 --> 00:04:27,847
Now, names for the stairs.

61
00:04:27,930 --> 00:04:31,366
A scale's first note is its tonic:
its home.

62
00:04:31,766 --> 00:04:37,573
Musicians number the notes up from home,
one to seven: the scale degrees.

63
00:04:37,656 --> 00:04:39,787
And they sing them with these names.

64
00:04:40,137 --> 00:04:46,364
Do, re, mi, fa, so, la, ti, and do again.

65
00:04:46,964 --> 00:04:53,671
TAVI: In the opening, do moved up to D.
But isn't do always C?

66
00:04:54,021 --> 00:04:57,918
MERC: In one system,
called fixed do, it is.

67
00:04:58,001 --> 00:05:03,582
We'll use movable do: the names
belong to the stairs, not the keys.

68
00:05:04,082 --> 00:05:11,313
Slide to D, and do is D,
re is E, mi is F sharp.

69
00:05:11,663 --> 00:05:14,529
Home moves, and the names move with it.

70
00:05:15,229 --> 00:05:17,146
Back to the opening question.

71
00:05:17,229 --> 00:05:22,656
Ti is the seventh note,
and it sits just a semitone below do:

72
00:05:22,739 --> 00:05:24,971
the last step is a short one.

73
00:05:25,471 --> 00:05:29,283
Listen: up to ti from C, and stop.

74
00:05:29,633 --> 00:05:33,533
[Merc sings up from C and stops on ti]

75
00:05:34,933 --> 00:05:39,280
Most listeners hear it
waiting for one more note.

76
00:05:39,363 --> 00:05:40,579
Take that step.

77
00:05:40,679 --> 00:05:42,579
[Merc sings the last step, up to do]

78
00:05:43,179 --> 00:05:46,107
TAVI: But why is do home, and not ti?

79
00:05:46,457 --> 00:05:51,404
MERC: In one experiment, listeners
heard a C major scale, then one more note,

80
00:05:51,487 --> 00:05:54,418
and rated how well it finished the scale.

81
00:05:54,768 --> 00:05:57,085
The top rating went to do.

82
00:05:57,168 --> 00:06:01,365
Later studies found the same kind of order
in listeners without training too,

83
00:06:01,448 --> 00:06:02,815
only weaker.

84
00:06:02,898 --> 00:06:07,689
So that sense of home seems to be learned,
just by hearing music.

85
00:06:08,289 --> 00:06:14,046
TAVI: So it sounds finished on do
because we've learned that do is home,

86
00:06:14,129 --> 00:06:17,578
and ti stops one short step under it?

87
00:06:17,928 --> 00:06:19,515
MERC: That's how it seems.

88
00:06:19,598 --> 00:06:24,955
Ti is called the leading note, because
it's thought of as leading to the tonic.

89
00:06:25,305 --> 00:06:33,360
It leads wherever home is. From D,
ti is C sharp, and it leans up to D.

90
00:06:36,260 --> 00:06:42,479
Let's sing it.
Listen first: up to ti, a wait, and home.

91
00:06:42,879 --> 00:06:51,479
[Merc sings up to ti,
waits, and lands on do]

92
00:06:52,079 --> 00:06:56,444
TAVI: So on ti, I just wait,
and listen for where it wants to go?

93
00:06:56,794 --> 00:06:59,231
MERC: Yes. Try it, an octave up.

94
00:06:59,631 --> 00:07:08,231
[Tavi sings up to ti, waits,
and lands on do, an octave higher]

95
00:07:08,831 --> 00:07:13,168
MERC: Now you.
Sore, hoarse or unwell today?

96
00:07:13,251 --> 00:07:14,615
Just listen along.

97
00:07:14,965 --> 00:07:21,732
Otherwise, sing on do, re, mi,
or on ooh, at about speaking volume.

98
00:07:21,815 --> 00:07:24,355
Start on my note, or an octave up.

99
00:07:24,705 --> 00:07:28,454
Wait on ti,
and notice where it wants to go.

100
00:07:28,804 --> 00:07:32,225
Stop immediately if
anything feels uncomfortable.

101
00:07:32,825 --> 00:07:33,742
[Merc sings do]

102
00:07:33,825 --> 00:07:42,425
[your turn: up to ti, wait, and do]

103
00:07:44,725 --> 00:07:45,653
Quick check.

104
00:07:46,003 --> 00:07:48,992
What pattern of steps makes a major scale?

105
00:07:49,342 --> 00:07:51,842
[think]

106
00:07:52,142 --> 00:07:58,109
Tone, tone, semitone,
tone, tone, tone, semitone.

107
00:07:58,192 --> 00:08:00,834
Two groups, joined by a tone.

108
00:08:01,434 --> 00:08:06,595
Start a major scale on D.
Which two black keys does it need?

109
00:08:06,945 --> 00:08:08,745
[think]

110
00:08:09,045 --> 00:08:10,923
F sharp and C sharp.

111
00:08:11,523 --> 00:08:15,869
From ti up to do: a tone, or a semitone?

112
00:08:16,219 --> 00:08:17,869
[ti, then do]

113
00:08:18,219 --> 00:08:20,019
[think]

114
00:08:20,319 --> 00:08:26,805
A semitone. Ti sits just under home,
and most listeners hear it lean up.

115
00:08:27,305 --> 00:08:32,559
TAVI: And in a major scale that
starts on G, which note is do?

116
00:08:32,909 --> 00:08:34,709
[think]

117
00:08:35,009 --> 00:08:39,101
MERC: G. Do is wherever
a major scale starts: its home.

118
00:08:39,601 --> 00:08:45,718
In MercuryPitch's Scale Runner, sing
a major scale back, one note at a time.

119
00:08:45,801 --> 00:08:50,731
Set your voice range in Settings first,
and sing in the octave it plays.

120
00:08:55,731 --> 00:08:56,608
Next time.

121
00:08:56,958 --> 00:09:00,407
Singers are told to "support" a long note.

122
00:09:00,807 --> 00:09:04,605
TAVI: If that doesn't mean pushing,
what keeps it steady?

123
00:09:05,005 --> 00:09:10,922
[chime]
